---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import ArticleSchema from '../../components/ArticleSchema.astro';
import { site, description } from '../../content';

const path = '/guides/two-way-sms';
const title = 'Two-Way SMS & Reply Handling';
const desc = description(site, 'guides/two-way-sms');


---
<BaseLayout title={title} description={desc} site={site} path={path}>
  <ArticleSchema title={title} description={desc} siteName={site.name} domain={site.domain} path={path} />
  <section class="container py-16">
    <a href="/guides" class="link">← All SMS guides</a>
    <h1 class="hero-title mt-5">{title}</h1>
    <div class="prose-shopify mt-8">
      <h2>A reply inbox needs an operating model</h2>
      <p>This guide is for support and lifecycle teams sharing a marketing number. Receiving replies is only the entry point: someone must recognize intent, prevent unwanted automation, answer accurately and close the loop. Define service coverage before inviting customers to ask questions by text. Do not promise immediate assistance if the queue is unattended.</p>
      <h2>Route by intent before assigning a sales task</h2>
      <p>Separate revocation requests, help requests, order issues, product questions and wrong-number reports. Treat ambiguous “please leave me alone” messages as a suppression review, not a sales objection. Keep provider-managed keyword handling separate from the human queue so automated suppression is not dependent on an agent opening a ticket. A HELP keyword and a request to cancel an order require different actions.</p>
      <h2>Scope conversational permission correctly</h2>
      <p>Twilio policy permits a direct response to an individual who initiates a conversation, but says that this does not establish permission for recurring engagement. Its policy also prohibits further messages after opt-out apart from a final confirmation unless fresh express written consent is provided. These are Twilio service rules, not a complete statement of US or international law. Keep marketing enrollment separate from resolving an inbound question.</p>
      <h2>Give each conversation an owner and a handoff</h2>
      <p>Record the inbound message ID, sender, relevant customer reference, category, assigned agent and next action. Deduplicate repeated webhook deliveries so one reply does not become two tickets. Transfer ownership explicitly between support and lifecycle teams. If the marketing platform and helpdesk both send, choose which may answer and prevent competing acknowledgments. Preserve the message history needed for the next agent to understand the issue.</p>
      <h2>Handle identity and sensitive requests</h2>
      <p>A phone-number match alone may not justify disclosing order details or changing an address. Move account changes and sensitive information to an authenticated support path. Do not ask for payment credentials in the text thread. For a shared or reassigned number, stop exposing previous-customer information and review the profile relationship. Limit agent access and retain only information needed for the support and applicable recordkeeping purpose.</p>
      <h2>Pause promotions while resolving a complaint</h2>
      <p>An open order complaint is a useful operational exclusion from unrelated offers even when the subscriber remains eligible for marketing. Define which ticket states pause which flows and who releases that pause. Closing a ticket should not recreate a revoked subscription. Keep service resolution separate from upsell scripts so a refund conversation cannot silently become a new promotion.</p>
      <h2>Set coverage and capacity from real queue data</h2>
      <p>Track unresolved age, first human response time, repeat contacts, transfers and reopened cases. Review distributions and busy periods rather than only an average. A launch that invites replies needs planned staffing and escalation coverage. If agents cannot handle the queue, change the invitation or campaign plan before adding volume. No universal reply-time target or staffing ratio is established by this guide.</p>
      <h2>Proposed reply-handling acceptance cases</h2>
      <p>Using authorized controlled fixtures, review a keyword opt-out, a natural-language revocation, an order cancellation, a duplicate inbound event, an after-hours inquiry and a shared-number case. Expect suppression or human review where appropriate, one assigned ticket, and no competing automated sales reply. Verify the outcome in both sender and helpdesk. These are proposed checks, not tests run on real subscribers for this page.</p>
      <h2>Evaluate two-way support beyond a feature checkbox</h2>
      <p>Ask the supplier to demonstrate assignment, keyword processing, history export, agent permissions and synchronization failures. Sequenzy advertises a paid-plan SMS/MMS beta with a US/Canada toll-free number; that alone does not establish the shared inbox and helpdesk behavior described here. Evaluate those requirements explicitly. A combined email/SMS product can simplify channel context only if the actual reply workflow supports the team.</p>
      <h2>Sources and related decisions</h2>
      <p>Supplier documentation checked November 25, 2026: <a href="https://www.sequenzy.com/pricing">Sequenzy current product scope</a>. <a href="https://www.twilio.com/en-us/legal/messaging-policy">Twilio messaging policy</a>. Product claims are scoped to these supplier statements; implementation and jurisdiction-specific requirements still need review.</p>
      <p><a href="/guides/consent">Consent</a> · <a href="/guides/email-sms-coordination">Email Sms Coordination</a> · <a href="/apps">App catalog</a></p>
    </div>
  </section>
</BaseLayout>
